nodecode-defuddle is a rewrite of annalinneajohansson82's annalinneajohansson82/hermes-defuddle:

  https://github.com/annalinneajohansson82/hermes-defuddle
  commit d28bd52eed30c397a45f0b2aba869a28ca4789c4
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 annalinneajohansson82 (the repository carries no
  LICENSE file; README.md and plugin.yaml declare MIT)

The rewrite is the plugin's web-extract provider as a Nodecode cell. What it
keeps: the program it drives, defuddle on PATH, and its argv ("parse" "--json"
URL, one URL per run); standard input at /dev/null, so the program can never
read a byte of the harness's own; a minute's deadline, with a process over it
killed rather than leaked; the same reading of the JSON it prints --
contentMarkdown as the article, title, description, site, domain, language,
wordCount, author and published as the metadata around it; the description
quoted line by line ahead of the article when it differs from the title; and no
raw HTML in the answer's place when the page produced no markdown. The plugin
took a list of URLs and ran them in parallel, checking nothing about any of
them; the cell reads one address per call and checks it first.

What is left out, and why: the provider interface. Hermes registered it as an
extract backend its web_extract tool picked from config; Nodecode ships no
extract registry -- its websearch cell reads pages itself -- so here the same
reader is a verb, a tool and a command instead of a backend. Parallel extraction
became one call per address, which is what a model calling a tool does anyway;
the model reads one page at a time and says so. The plugin's README noted an
SSRF post-redirect gap (defuddle follows redirects internally, so the URL it
ends on is never re-checked); that gap was the host's to close and is unchanged
here, with one addition of this cell's own: an address that is not http:// or
https:// is refused before the program sees it, so the address can never be
read as one of defuddle's flags.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell runs the same defuddle
program the plugin ran, on PATH, and its own code is Common Lisp.
